challenges.description:
What is the type of `gen` here, and what happens each time you call `.next()`?

  function* counter(start: number): Generator<number> {
    let i = start
    while (true) { yield i++ }
  }

  const gen = counter(1)

What are the three type parameters of `Generator<T, TReturn, TNext>`?

  function* adder(): Generator<number, string, number> {
    let total = 0
    while (true) {
      const n = yield total
      if (n === undefined) return 'done'
      total += n
    }
  }

What is the difference between `Iterable<T>` and `Iterator<T>`?

  // Which does for...of require?
  for (const x of someValue) { ... }

  // Which does .next() live on?

What must a class implement to be usable with `for...of`?

  class Range {
    constructor(private start: number, private end: number) {}
    // what goes here?
  }

  for (const n of new Range(1, 5)) { ... }

Write a `LinkedList<T>` class that is iterable:

  interface ListNode<T> { value: T; next: ListNode<T> | null }

  class LinkedList<T> implements Iterable<T> {
    private head: ListNode<T> | null = null

    push(value: T): void        // add to end
    toArray(): T[]              // collect all values
    [Symbol.iterator](): Iterator<T>  // enables for...of
  }

The iterator must walk the linked list from head to tail. Use a generator function for the iterator implementation.
